weaknesses.issue:
The paper is not easy to follow due to a number of reasons: 
- many notations are not clearly defined and some of them may be used interchangeably, e.g., S, s, \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{F}, \mathcal{D}, \mathcal{R}, \mathcal{M}; 
- some sentences are unclear, e.g., "we employ both an L1 loss for the blended texture and a geometric loss for the decoded strand geometry". It is unclear what "blended texture" means; 
- some figures are not well explained, e.g., Fig. 2, it is unclear what the colors represent; 
- some technical details are missing, e.g., how the inverse DFT in Eq. (1) is computed. 

The paper claims that the proposed method is able to capture both low- and high-frequency information, but it is unclear how this is achieved. It is also unclear how the frequencies are defined.
